WEBVTT
TITLE AVL Trees: the Balance Invariant, the Four Rotations and the Height Bound — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Six keys. Inserted in sorted order they make a chain five levels deep, and a search may visit every one of them.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The same six keys, inserted as 9, 6, 5, 8, 7, 10, sit in height 2. Every search now visits at most three nodes.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
An AVL tree keeps the height logarithmic no matter what order the keys arrive in. That is the whole promise.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
It buys that with one extra rule, and the rule is local: at every node, the two subtree heights differ by at most one.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Height is a convention before it is a number. An empty tree has height minus one; a single node has height zero.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The balance factor of a node is the height of its right subtree minus the height of its left subtree.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The AVL condition is that this factor lies between minus one and plus one, at every node, at all times.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A node whose factor reaches plus or minus two is the only thing a rotation exists to fix.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Insert 30, then 20, then 10. Node 30 ends up with factor minus two: two levels of left child in a row.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
One right rotation at 30 fixes it. The middle key 20 rises, and 10 and 30 become its children.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The mirror case is RR: 10, 20, 30 gives factor plus two at 10, and one left rotation lifts 20.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Both single cases move three pointers. Nothing is copied, nothing is allocated, and the in-order walk is untouched.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Now insert 30, then 10, then 20. The path goes left and then right: a zig-zag, not a straight line.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
A right rotation at 30 alone would leave the zig-zag intact. So first rotate left at the child 10.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Now the case is LL, so rotate right at 30. Two rotations, and the same tree as the single cases.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So a double rotation is two single rotations, and both must be counted if the question asks for a count.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Take 30 with left child 20, and 20 has a right child 25. Node 30 has factor minus two.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
A right rotation is three assignments: name the left child, hand its right subtree to the old root, hang the old root beneath it.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The in-order walk before and after is 20 25 30 both times. That is the proof the search property survives.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
So the repair is O(1) on top of an O(log n) descent. The rotation never dominates the cost.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Insert 9, then 6. Nothing breaks: 9 leans left by one, which is legal.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Insert 5 and 9 reaches minus two. LL at 9, so one right rotation: 6 becomes the root.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Insert 8, then 7. Now 8 has a left child 7 beneath 9, so 9 breaks again: LL, one more rotation.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Insert 10 and the root 6 leans right, so one left rotation: 8 becomes the root at height 2.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The six-key run needed three repairs: none, none, one, none, one, one.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Why one is enough for an insertion: the repaired subtree comes back to its old height, so no ancestor can break.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
A deletion is different: the repaired subtree can come back shorter, so the imbalance travels up the path.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
So deletion costs up to O(log n) rotations and insertion O(1). Both are still O(log n) in total.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
To bound the height, ask the reverse question: how few nodes can a legal tree of height h have?

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
So N of h is N of h minus one, plus N of h minus two, plus one for the root: 1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
A 7-key AVL tree can therefore be three tall, and no taller: N of 3 is exactly 7.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Solving the recurrence gives height at most 1.44 log base 2 of n plus 2, minus a third: logarithmic, with a constant near 1.44.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
This tree on the left is AVL and is not complete. Nine keys, height 3, every factor inside the window.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
And this one has a perfectly balanced root, factor zero, while two nodes below it are off by two.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Three different promises: perfect is a shape with all levels full, complete fills the last level from the left, AVL only bounds heights.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So the exam-safe check is a bottom-up pass: compute every height from the leaves, then test every factor in one walk.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The routine, four steps: draw the insertions in the given order, mark heights and factors as you go.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Then repair at the lowest node with factor plus or minus two, using its child and grandchild sides to name the case.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap one: repairing at the root when the lowest break is deeper. Trap two: one rotation on a zig-zag.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Trap three: reading balanced as complete, or checking only the root. Trap four: using the perfect-tree formula for minimum nodes.

